! Silicon, eight atoms: the conventional cubic cell of the diamond structure.
!
! The same crystal as si-1k.in seen in a different cell -- the primitive fcc cell
! has two atoms, the cubic one that contains it has eight -- so the physics is
! known and only the size changes. Four times the atoms, four times the volume,
! four times the plane waves and four times the bands, which is where the cost
! of a real calculation starts to look like itself: the two-atom cases are small
! enough that fixed overheads dominate both codes.
!
! One k-point, as in the other benchmarks: both codes parallelise over k, so a
! multi-k comparison measures batching rather than the cost of the physics.
 &control
    calculation = 'scf'
 /
 &system
    ibrav=1, celldm(1) =10.20,
    nat=8, ntyp=1,
    ecutwfc=12.0
 /
 &electrons
    conv_thr = 1.0d-10
 /
ATOMIC_SPECIES
 Si  28.086  Si.pz-vbc.UPF
ATOMIC_POSITIONS (alat)
 Si 0.00 0.00 0.00
 Si 0.00 0.50 0.50
 Si 0.50 0.00 0.50
 Si 0.50 0.50 0.00
 Si 0.25 0.25 0.25
 Si 0.25 0.75 0.75
 Si 0.75 0.25 0.75
 Si 0.75 0.75 0.25
K_POINTS
  1
   0.250000  0.250000  0.250000   1.00
